(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-dna-viruses-of-medical-importance-quiz"]={"title":"DNA Viruses of Medical Importance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement about the transmission of genital herpes is accurate?","choices":["It spreads mainly by respiratory droplets","It spreads mainly through blood products","It can spread without visible lesions","It needs visible active lesions to spread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Herpes simplex spreads by direct exposure to secretions containing the virus. Active lesions are the most significant source, but genital herpes can be transmitted in the absence of lesions, so herpes simplex virus type 2 can spread without visible lesions. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 58, 59, 68 and 87 and image on Slide 5"},{"q":"Most DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) viruses are budded off which part of the host cell?","choices":["The cytoplasm","The cell wall","An endocytic vesicle","The nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) viruses are budded off the nucleus, while most RNA (ribonucleic acid) viruses multiply in and are released from the cytoplasm. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 24 and 22 and image on Slide 23"},{"q":"How does roseola from human herpesvirus 6 typically unfold in a baby?","choices":["Vesicles with malaise and fever","Fever first, then a faint rash","A faint rash first, then fever","A cheek rash with low fever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Roseola is an acute febrile disease of babies 2 to 12 months old: it begins with fever, followed by a faint maculopapular rash, and is usually self-limited. Adults infected with human herpesvirus 6 may instead get mono-like symptoms, lymphadenopathy and hepatitis. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 81 and 104 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"How do humans usually catch orf virus?","choices":["Inhaling airborne saliva","Keeping pet prairie dogs","Handling sheep and goats","Handling rodent-hunting cats"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Orf virus is a parapox virus mainly of ruminant animals, sheep and goats, in which it causes sores on the mouth. People catch it by handling the animals: feeding, shearing and bites. It is more serious in the immunocompromised. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 54, 40, 49 and 53"},{"q":"How does latent herpesvirus DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) differ from a phage prophage?","choices":["It is detectable in tissue","It is not integrated","It lies in the cytoplasm","It can never reactivate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A prophage is phage DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) integrated into the bacterial chromosome. Latent herpesvirus DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is an episome, in the nucleus but not integrated, though both lie dormant and can later reactivate into active virus production. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 26, 15 and 27"},{"q":"Which skin condition may be a risk factor for molluscum contagiosum, through breaks in the skin?","choices":["Cold sores","Shingles","Atopic dermatitis","Plantar warts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atopic dermatitis may be a risk factor for molluscum contagiosum because of the breaks it leaves in the skin. The virus spreads by direct contact and fomites and causes small, smooth, waxy lesions in the genital area and on the thighs. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 46, 58, 69 and 97"},{"q":"Which stimulus can trigger a recurrence of a latent herpes simplex virus type 1 infection?","choices":["Eating salt-cured foods","Handling sheep or goats","Coinfection with malaria","Ultraviolet radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent herpes simplex infection is triggered by stimuli such as fever, ultraviolet radiation, stress and mechanical injury. Newly formed viruses then migrate from the ganglion to the body surface and produce a local skin or membrane lesion, such as a cold sore. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 54, 59 and 77"},{"q":"A newborn's mother is infected with hepatitis B virus. What gives the newborn passive immunization?","choices":["Delivery by cesarean section","An adeno-associated virus vector","Hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG)","The yeast-derived surface antigen vaccine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Passive immunization with hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG) is given to people exposed or possibly exposed, including neonates born to infected mothers and health care workers. Vaccination, the primary prevention, is active immunity and is encouraged for all newborns. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 64, 91 and 106"},{"q":"At the end of the phage lytic cycle, how are the new virions released?","choices":["Lysozyme bursts the wall","Extrusion without lysis","Budding off the membrane","Induction by a shock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Release is the last lytic step: viral lysozyme causes the host cell wall to burst, freeing the new virions and destroying the host cell. Destruction of the host cell is one of the defining features of the lytic cycle. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 14, 15, 18 and 22"},{"q":"Mpox virus enters the body through broken skin, mucous membranes, or which other route?","choices":["The respiratory tract","Intact skin","The placenta","The fifth cranial nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mpox virus enters through broken skin, the respiratory tract or mucous membranes, by contact with infected animals or humans, their body fluids, or fomites. Fever, aches, chills, swollen lymph nodes and exhaustion follow, then the rash. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 48, 28 and 59 and image on Slide 4"},{"q":"Adeno-associated virus is a defective parvovirus. What does it need to replicate in a host cell?","choices":["Co-infection with adenovirus","Co-infection with hepatitis B virus","Co-infection with BK polyomavirus","A bacterial host cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adeno-associated virus is a defective parvovirus: it cannot replicate in the host cell without adenovirus. By itself it is not known to cause disease, although co-infection with an adenovirus that causes hepatitis may make that infection more severe. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 13, 88, 102 and 106"},{"q":"How is parvovirus B19 transmitted?","choices":["Droplet infection","Blood or serum contact","Direct skin contact","Contaminated food"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parvovirus B19 spreads by droplet infection. It causes fifth disease in children, with a slapped-face rash and low-grade fever, hydrops fetalis when passed to a fetus, and polyarthropathy in adults. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 6 and image on Slide 7"},{"q":"Why was variolation replaced by vaccination?","choices":["It caused facial tumor growths","It infected pet prairie dogs","It ended herd immunity to pox","It seeded new smallpox cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Variolation did not always work and sometimes failed to protect during an epidemic, and the collateral smallpox cases spread by variolated people began to outweigh its benefits. Vaccination was less harmful, and variolation was made illegal in some countries. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 38, 46, 49 and 53"},{"q":"How do adenoviruses mainly spread from person to person?","choices":["Contact with saliva alone","Respiratory and ocular secretions","Blood and sexual contact","Direct skin contact and fomites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenoviruses spread by respiratory and ocular secretions (droplet infection), which fits where they infect: respiratory epithelium and the conjunctiva, causing colds, pharyngitis and conjunctivitis. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 6 and 93 and image on Slide 7"},{"q":"Which description fits a herpesvirus virion and its deoxyribonucleic acid genome?","choices":["Nonenveloped, single-stranded","Nonenveloped, circular, double-stranded","Enveloped, partly single-stranded","Enveloped, icosahedral, double-stranded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Herpesviruses are large, enveloped, icosahedral viruses with double-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA). The virion has glycoprotein spikes on its envelope, a tegument layer beneath it, and a capsid holding the deoxyribonucleic acid core. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 55, 56, 86, 96 and 103"},{"q":"Most cytomegalovirus infections are asymptomatic. Which group develops a more virulent form of the disease?","choices":["Wrestlers and rugby players","Healthy older adults","Immunodeficient adults","College-age young adults"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three groups develop a more virulent cytomegalovirus disease: fetuses, newborns and immunodeficient adults. In acquired immunodeficiency syndrome it causes retinitis and disseminated disease, and most transplant patients get pneumonitis or hepatitis; treatment is reserved for the immunocompromised. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 62, 69, 72, 74 and 75"},{"q":"Poxviruses show specificity for the cytoplasm of which cells, which leads to their pustules?","choices":["Epidermal cells","Trigeminal ganglion cells","B lymphocytes","Liver cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Poxviruses have a specificity for the cytoplasm of epidermal cells and subcutaneous connective tissue. That is what produces their eruptive skin pustules, the pocks or pox that leave scars, and it can also stimulate tumor formation. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 32, 75, 77, 59 and 87"},{"q":"Which type of infection does hepatitis B virus establish, with virus detectable in tissue and multiplying slowly?","choices":["Acute lytic","Lysogenic","Persistent chronic","Persistent latent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic infections keep the virus detectable in tissue samples, multiplying at a slow rate, with mild or absent symptoms; hepatitis B virus and human immunodeficiency virus are the examples. Chronic infection may involve integration of viral genetic material into the host chromosome. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"An enveloped animal virus attaches to its host cell. Which viral structure binds the cell receptors?","choices":["Its matrix","Its spikes","Its tail fibers","Its nucleic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In adsorption the virus attaches to its host cell by specific binding of its spikes to cell receptors. This receptor fit is also why viruses are limited to a particular host or cell type. To tell them apart: tail fibers are phage parts; spikes are animal virus parts. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 9, 10 and 24 and image on Slide 22"},{"q":"A pregnant woman with genital herpes has an active outbreak when labor begins. Which delivery is advised to protect the infant?","choices":["Vaginal delivery, then infant screening","Delivery by cesarean section","Vaginal delivery after vaccination","Vaginal delivery once lesions crust"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Herpes of the newborn is prevented by screening pregnant women and by delivery by cesarean section if there is an outbreak at the time of birth, because the infant is otherwise contaminated by the mother during birth. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 58, 59, 60 and 64 and image on Slide 4"},{"q":"Nine human papillomavirus types increase the risk of cancer in which body system?","choices":["Reproductive system","Hepatic system","Lymphatic system","Nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nine human papillomavirus types increase the risk of developing reproductive system cancer; papillomaviruses are linked to cervical and penile cancer. Women are screened by Pap smear for abnormal cervical cells. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 28, 87, 99 and 102 and image on Slide 7"},{"q":"Patients with Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis show a shift in gut virome composition. What does this shift establish?","choices":["Phages protect the bowel","A link, cause not proven","Phages cause the disease","The disease destroys phages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Some studies show a shift in virome composition in inflammatory bowel disease, Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis, but this may or may not be a cause-and-effect relationship. It is an observed association, not proof that phages cause the disease. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"After initial infection through the tonsils or gastrointestinal tract, where does JC polyomavirus remain latent?","choices":["Gut and kidney tubular cells","The trigeminal ganglion","Liver cells","Lymphoid tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After initial infection via the tonsils or gastrointestinal tract, JC virus is latent in the gastrointestinal tract and the tubular epithelial cells of the kidneys, shedding virus in urine. It can cross the blood-brain barrier in immunosuppressed patients. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 59, 87, 93 and 102"},{"q":"A bacteriophage has a head joined to a tail with a sheath and tail fibers. Which capsid type is this?","choices":["Enveloped","Complex","Helical","Icosahedral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Viral capsids come in three shapes: helical, icosahedral and complex, and the complex shape is the phage type. The head-and-tail build, with sheath, plug and tail fibers below a capsid head, is what makes a phage capsid complex rather than a simple rod or a many-sided solid. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 9 and 11"},{"q":"Of the three principal viruses that cause hepatitis, which one is a deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) virus?","choices":["Hepatitis A virus","Hepatitis C virus","Hepatitis D virus","Hepatitis B virus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hepatitis B virus is the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) virus among the three principal hepatitis viruses; hepatitis A and hepatitis C viruses are ribonucleic acid (RNA) viruses. It belongs to the hepadnaviruses, enveloped deoxyribonucleic acid viruses with a tropism for the liver. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 85, 86 and 88"},{"q":"Besides a family history, which risk factor is listed for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, an Epstein-Barr virus-associated tumor?","choices":["Eating salt-cured foods","Playing contact sports","Handling sheep or goats","Receiving a kidney transplant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a malignancy of epithelial cells occurring in adults, older Chinese and African men, those with a family history and those who eat salt-cured foods. It presents with a lump in the neck, hearing loss and a blocked nasal passage. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 54, 62, 77 and 102 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"During latency, how does a herpesvirus infection differ from a persistent chronic infection?","choices":["The virus multiplies slowly in tissue","The host cell is destroyed quickly","Its genes replicate as a prophage","The virus is generally not detectable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a latent infection, after a lytic cycle, the herpesvirus enters a dormant phase: it is generally not detectable and causes no symptoms, yet it can reactivate later. In a chronic infection the virus stays detectable in tissue samples, multiplying slowly. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 15, 26 and 55"},{"q":"Epstein-Barr virus is oncogenic. Which cancer is it linked with?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Hepatic carcinoma","Cervical cancer","Burkitt lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among persistent oncogenic viruses, Epstein-Barr virus is linked with Burkitt lymphoma. The other pairings are human herpesvirus 8 with Kaposi sarcoma, hepatitis B virus with hepatic carcinoma, and papillomaviruses with cervical and penile cancer. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 28, 6 and 30"},{"q":"What is the natural host range of varicella zoster virus?","choices":["Humans and rodents","Humans and sheep","Humans and cattle","Humans only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varicella zoster virus, the cause of chickenpox and shingles, has humans as its only natural host. It passes from person to person by respiratory droplets and by contact with fluid from active skin lesions. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 53, 54 and 68"},{"q":"Why is a given virus limited to a particular host or cell type?","choices":["Presence of an envelope","Mode of transmission","Receptor compatibility","Genome size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Viruses are limited to a particular host or cell type by compatibility of receptors and genetic material. At adsorption the virus attaches by specific binding of its spikes to the cell receptors, so cells without a matching receptor are not infected. — Lecture 18  Dr. Webster -  DNA viruses of medical importance.pptx, Slide 24 and image on Slide 22"}]};
